            "text": "{\n  \"judgments\": [\n    {\n      \"proposal_id\": \"P0002\",\n      \"source_refs\": [\"F001\", \"F002\", \"F003\"],\n      \"rationale\": \"The existing draft claim C0000 accurately and completely represents 49 U.S.C. 30120(c)(1), preserving the actor, conditional trigger, mandatory modal force, and alternative remedy actions without error or omission. The separate evidentiary rule, extension authority, and presentation conditions in paragraph (c)(2) are distinct provisions already covered in separate claims, so no change to C0000 is required.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"supported\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"proposal_id\": \"P0000\",\n      \"source_refs\": [\"F001\", \"F002\", \"F003\", \"F005\"],\n      \"rationale\": \"This edit is unnecessary because C0000 is already complete and faithful to paragraph (c)(1). Incorporating the distinct evidentiary presumption, administrative extension authority, and presentation rules of paragraph (c)(2) into the summary of paragraph (c)(1) conflates distinct statutory provisions that are separately captured.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"unsupported\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"proposal_id\": \"P0001\",\n      \"source_refs\": [\"F001\", \"F002\", \"F003\", \"F005\"],\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal misstates the statutory text by expanding the refund remedy in subparagraph (B) to 'either a vehicle or replacement equipment', whereas the statute explicitly limits the refund option to 'for a vehicle'. It also unnecessarily merges the separate rules of paragraph (c)(2) into this claim.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"unsupported\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
